<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 983–<a href="/years/1009">1009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Sanchuelo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> by a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ii-of-pamplona">Sancho II of Pamplona</a>, hence his nickname 'Little Sancho'. He succeeded his brother Abd al-Malik as hajib of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1008 and persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> to name him heir to the caliphate. The move provoked a revolt in Córdoba in 1009, and he was killed, setting off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus">Fitna of al-Andalus</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abd al-Rahman, who took the name al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> to the hajib <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> and Abda, a converted daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ii-of-pamplona">Sancho II of Pamplona</a> who was probably first named Urraca or Sancha. He grew up at his father's residence of az-Zahira, and at about nine, in September 992, he rode out to escort his visiting grandfather Sancho II to Almanzor's court. His father set out to harden him as a warrior: according to the poet Ibn Darraj, around 995 he was made to behead one of 50 Pamplonan captives, his own uncle, in reprisal for an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a>. He joined the campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in 997 and fought on the front line at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cervera">Cervera</a> in 1000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>'s death in 1002 his brother Abd al-Malik al-Muzaffar became hajib and campaigned against the Christian north; at the surrender of San Martín in 1007 Sanchuelo was ordered to kill the men among the hostages and enslave the women and children. When al-Muzaffar died in October 1008, many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> believed Abd al-Rahman had poisoned him. Chroniclers condemned his debauchery, drinking and foolishness, and his proclamation as heir in November 1008, won with the help of the qadi of Córdoba, set the middle class and the populace against him, though the mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Slav army stayed loyal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ignoring a warning of an Umayyad plot, he left in mid-January 1009 on a winter campaign against León and its boy-king Alfonso V, but snow, mud and floods drove his army back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. On 15 February 30 armed men led by Muhammad ibn Hisham ibn Abd al-Jabbar, a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>, seized the palace; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> abdicated in Muhammad's favour, and az-Zahira was looted to rubble.</p><p>Sanchuelo dithered at Calatrava as his troops failed to renew their oaths, then marched on the capital with his ally García Gómez, count of Carrión, while his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> army deserted. Surrounded at Armilat, he was being taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> when he drew a dagger in what was read as a suicide attempt and was beheaded on 4 March 1009. His fall encouraged other Umayyad scions and local lords to rise, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> broke into dozens of warring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Almanzor’s son Abd al-Malik al-Muzaffar succeeded him as hajib in 1002 and continued his campaigns, but on his death in 1008 his brother Abd al-Rahman, nicknamed <b>Sanchuelo</b>, persuaded Hisham II to name him heir to the caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=999" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4678 4321h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4678 4321h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=999">What was here in 999 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
